    {
      "ply": 38,
      "san": "Qxe4",
      "human_san": "Qxe4",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "\"I could not believe\nthat in such an important tournament I could have the opportunity to sacrifice\nthe Queen. If somebody has told me that I could have such an opportunity\nagainst Capablanca...\" (Lilienthal)",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "19...Qxe4 concedes a winning advantage to White (+0.08 \u2192 +1.88, Stockfish:16), and exf6 is the refutation that punishes it along [pv:Qxe4 exf6 Nxf6 Qxe4+ Nxe4]; now White's pawn structure is improved, a steady edge once exchanges settle. Black missed the potential to target the rook on f1 and activate the rooks on the open files with 19...Nxe5, which leads to equality [pv:Nxe5 Bxf6 gxf6 Nf4 Rd8].",
      "system_symbol": "?",
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": true,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": false,
      "neither": false,
      "text_ratio": 0.0297,
      "text_jaccard": 0.0
    },
    {
      "ply": 39,
      "san": "exf6",
      "human_san": "exf6",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "Very good move! To remember. A move suggested by the position of the Black\nKing, which is quite open, and the temporary paralysis of opponent's Rooks and\nKnight.",
      "human_symbol": "!",
      "system_comment": "White's most accurate move is exf6, maintaining the advantage (+1.92, Stockfish:16) as the continuation [pv:exf6 Nxf6 Qxe4+ Nxe4 Ng3] may leave the king safer\u2014always valuable when central files open\u2014and could leave Black with a hanging piece. Qxe4 is clearly weaker\u2014it leads only to a slight advantage for White [pv:Qxe4 Nxe4 Nf4 O-O Rae1], leaving Black's pieces passive without creating the same problems for Black.",
      "system_symbol": "!",
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": true,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": false,
      "neither": false,
      "text_ratio": 0.0208,
      "text_jaccard": 0.0545,
      "symbol_agreement": "exact"
    },
    {
      "ply": 40,
      "san": "Qxc2",
      "human_san": "Qxc2",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "Black missed the chance to play 20...Nxf6, which gives White a winning advantage [pv:Nxf6 Qxe4+ Nxe4 Ng3 Nd6], and instead captured on c2 with 20...Qxc2 \u2014 a move that makes matters worse, as White now has a winning advantage (+1.92 \u2192 +2.48, Stockfish:16) [pv:Qxc2 fxg7 Rg8 Nd4 Qd2]. Deeper in the line, Black's pawn structure may grow weaker, with doubled, isolated, and backward pawns that can tie pieces to their defense.",
      "system_symbol": "?!",
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": false,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": true,
      "neither": false
    },
    {
      "ply": 41,
      "san": "fxg7",
      "human_san": "fxg7",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "The best move, 21.fxg7, maintains White's advantage (+2.45, Stockfish:16) by creating a passed pawn on g7 that can tie down Black's pieces. The continuation [pv:fxg7 Rg8 Nd4 Qd2 Rae1+] may build mounting pressure on the black king. In return, Black holds a queen for a bishop and pawn, and White leaves a piece hanging. Rac1 is clearly weaker, giving Black a winning advantage with [pv:Rac1 Qxc1 Rxc1 Nxf6 Bxf6], where White could shed doubled pawns only for Black to pick up doubled f-pawns deeper in the line.",
      "system_symbol": "!",
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": false,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": true,
      "neither": false
    },
