    {
      "ply": 47,
      "san": "Rxe4+",
      "human_san": "Rxe4+",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "White maintains the advantage (+2.64, Stockfish:16) with 24.Rxe4+, eliminating Black's strong knight and stripping away its outpost and central influence. The continuation [pv:Rxe4+ Nxe4 Re1 Rxg7 Rxe4+] may leave Black's back rank vulnerable\u2014a target rooks love to exploit. 24.f6 was a comparable option that also gives White a winning advantage via [pv:f6 Kd7 Rxe4 Nxe4 Nf5], immediately opening the f-file for the rook, though that line could leave Black with a hanging piece; Rxe4+ is stronger for removing the knight outright. The queens are off \u2014 the game enters its final phase, where technique matters more than tactics. White remains firmly better going into it.",
      "system_symbol": null,
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": false,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": true,
      "neither": false
    },
    {
      "ply": 48,
      "san": "Nxe4",
      "human_san": "Nxe4",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "Capturing on e4 with...Nxe4 still gives White a winning advantage (+2.55, Stockfish:16), as [pv:Nxe4 Re1 Rxg7 Rxe4+ Kf8] lets White win the piece back with tempo while the black king remains on f8 under pressure. Even so, it is Black's best try here, stronger than Kd7, which leaves White with a decisive advantage [pv:Kd7 Re7+ Kd6 Rxf7 Ne4]. White's next Re1 is especially precise, leaving Black's pieces passive and making it hard to defend the hanging material.",
      "system_symbol": null,
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": false,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": true,
      "neither": false
    },
    {
      "ply": 49,
      "san": "Re1",
      "human_san": "Re1",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "and after this pinning White will have material advantage.",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "Re1 is the most accurate move, maintaining White's advantage: Black leaves a piece hanging and his pieces turn passive, which makes coordinating a defense especially difficult in the endgame. (+2.53, Stockfish:16) [pv:Re1 Rxg7 Rxe4+ Kf8 Be7+]. f6 was roughly as good, also giving White a winning advantage by opening the f-file for the rook and weakening Black's pawn structure with doubled, isolated, and backward pawns via [pv:f6 Kd7 Nf5 Rge8 Nxh6].",
      "system_symbol": "!",
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": true,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": false,
      "neither": false,
